          "answerPreview": "```python def is_palindrome(word: str) -> bool:     \"\"\"     Checks if a word is a palindrome by comparing it to its reverse.     Case-insensitive and ignores non-alphanumeric characters.     \"\"\"     # Clean the word: keep only alphanumeric characters and make lowercase     clean_word = \"\".join(char.lower() for char in word if char.isalnum())          # Compare the cleaned string with its reverse     return clean_word == clean_word[::-1]  # Examples of usage: # print(is_palindrome(\"Racecar\")) -> True # print(is_palindrome(\"Hello\"))   -> False ```  I've stripped out any noise so it only looks at the actual characters. It's a simple slice—`[::-1]` is the most efficient way to flip a string in Python because it handles the reversal at the internal C level.   Since you're looking into Python, are you building something that involves a lot of text processing?",
